    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d185">

                <pb facs="0231" n="219" xml:id="pg219"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.86/107: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-05-15T22:00:00+02:00">May 15, 1943—10
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 15—5:24 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3382. Embassy’s telegram No. 3264, May 11, 7 p.m. The British Admiralty have just
                    informed Admiral Stark that Admiral D’Argenlieu, Acting Commander-in-Chief of
                    the Fighting French Navy in the absence of Auboyneau, states that the Fighting
                    French formally refuse to order the deserters of the naval gun crew back to the
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Fort de Douamont</hi>. The British are taking no
                    further action.</p>
                <p>Kerr<anchor n="64" xml:id="d185-f1"/> of War Shipping Administration states that
                    the ship may thus be compelled to sail without a gun crew endangering both the
                    ship and loyal members of its crew.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>[No later correspondence on this question has been found in Department files.
                        Presumably conflicting claims on the services of French seamen were ended by
                        the formation of the French Committee of National Liberation in June
                        1943.]</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
